<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#military-junta">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Military junta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1170">1170 CE</a> – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A military junta is government by a council of officers, usually after a coup. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>’s Goryeo generals ruled this way from 1170, and juntas spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after 1945.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the reformist coup of October 1979, civilian–<b>military juntas</b> and then Duarte, elected president in 1984, governed under strong US pressure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Argentina's <b>military junta</b> fell soon after.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-14">14 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A tank broke through the gates of the Athens Polytechnic, ending a student occupation against the <b>military junta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-17">17 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
